    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In Cache Valley within Arches National Park, Utah, small faults developed in sandstone via several macroscopic deformation mechanisms during a short period of activity. Fieldwork indicates a complex faulting history including distinct stages dominated first by deformation banding and later by jointing. Linkage of fault segments played a significant role in fault development. While active the faults studied had a significant impact on fluid flow complicated by the presence of different deformation mechanisms. The potential impact on fluid flow changed on this fault over time corresponding with the change in deformation mechanism.\r\n\r\nStudying small normal faults in sandstone provides the opportunity to characterize the development and interaction of faulting mechanisms during fault formation. The work has two important objectives. (1) To evaluate the distribution of these mechanisms along the fault trace and the implications of this variation on normal fault development and linkage. (2) To evaluate the interaction of deformation banding and jointing during normal fault formation and development. This interaction controls the formation the localization of deformation that produces a discrete fault zone. Field observation indicates that the occurrence of different mechanisms and their spatial distribution is an important characteristic for assessing the effect of faults on fluid flow in the subsurface.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
